#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# compiled_info

#% text
# `compiled_info(f)` describes the compiled specialisation of `f`, as a list of rules.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The rules give the parameter and result types (`params`, `result`), the sizes of the intermediate forms (`hir_nodes`, `lir_instructions`) and of the code (`code_bytes`), the `back_end`, the compile time, the functions `inlined`, the `state` (`"pending"`, `"active"` or `"stale"`), and how many calls ran compiled (`runs`) or fell back to the definition (`declines`).

#% item
# A function that was never compiled gives `failure(not_compiled, f)`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
fn sq(x: Real) = x*x + 1

#% cell
compiled_info(sq)
#% output
#! failure(not_compiled, sq)

#% cell
compile(sq)
#% output
#= sq

#% cell
[params, result, hir_nodes, state] /. compiled_info(sq)
#% output
#= [[Real], Real, 6, "active"]

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Calls are counted:

#% cell
sq(1.0); sq(2.0); runs /. compiled_info(sq)
#% output
#= 2

#% text
# A call with an exact argument runs the definition and is not counted as compiled:

#% cell
sq(2); runs /. compiled_info(sq)
#% output
#= 2

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `compile`
